The distance from the origin to these three points is: \begin{align*}
3 \sqrt{4} &= 6, \quad 2 \sqrt{2}, \quad 2 \sqrt{2} + t.
\end{align*} Since the center of the hyperbola is at the origin, the foci are at a distance of $6$ or $2 \sqrt{2}$ from the origin. The two points having a distance from the origin of $2 \sqrt{2}$ define the transverse axis. When the hyperbola opens vertically, the vertices are at $(0, -2 \sqrt{2})$ and $(0, 2 \sqrt{2})$; when the hyperbola opens horizontally, the vertices are at $(-6, 0)$ and $(6,0)$. The line passing through these two points has the equation $y = \frac{1}{3}x$. The point of intersection is $(-3, 2)$, which lies on the circle $(x^2 + y^2) = 6^2$. However, $(t, 2)$ would also lie on the circle if $|t| = 2$. In fact, the hyperbola passes through the point $(t, 2)$, so $\cases{} & \mbox{ if }t<0 \mbox{,}\\ t &= \mathrm{-} 2 \mbox{,} \\ & \mbox{ if }t>0 \mbox{,}\\ t &= \mathrm{2}. \end{cases}$ Then $t^2 = 4$.

