Let $B = \pi - A - C$, so $\sin B = \sin(A+C)$ and $\cos B = -\cos(A+C)$.
We have \begin{align*}
\cos^2 B &= \cos^2(A+C) = \cos^2 A + \cos^2 C + 2 \cos A \cos C \cos(A+C), \\
2 \sin B \sin C \cos A &= 2 \sin(A+C) \sin C \cos A = 2(\sin A \cos C + \cos A \sin C) \sin C \cos A = 2 \sin A \sin C \cos^2 A + 2 \sin^2 C \sin A \cos A.
\end{align*}
Adding these together, we get \begin{align*}
2 \sin(A+C) \sin C \cos A &= 2 \cos^2 A \sin A \sin C + 2 \cos^2 C \sin C \sin A + 2 \cos A \cos C \cos(A+C) + 2 \sin A \sin C \cos^2 A \\
&= 2 \sin C \cos^2 A (1 + \cos A \cos B) + 2 \sin C \sin A \cos^2 C + 2 \cos C \sin A \cos C \cos A.
\end{align*}
Since $A+B+C = \pi$, we have $\cos A \cos B = \cos A \sin(A+C)$.
Using the identity $\sin C = \sin A \cos C + \cos A \sin C$, we can simplify the expression to get \begin{align*}
2 \sin(A+C) \sin C \cos A &= \frac{17}{18} \cos(A+C) = \frac{17}{18} (-\cos B).
\end{align*}
Substituting this back into the original equation for $\cos^2 A + \cos^2 B + 2 \sin A \sin B \cos C$, we get
\begin{align*}
\cos^2 B + \cos^2 C + 2 \sin B \sin C \cos A
&= \frac{17}{18} (-\cos B) + 2 \sin B \sin C \cos A \\
&= \frac{15}{8}.
\end{align*}
Solving this equation for $\sin B \sin C \cos A$, we get
\begin{align*}
\sin B \sin C \cos A &= 5.
\end{align*}
Similarly, solving the equation for $\cos^2 B + \cos^2 C + 2 \sin B \sin C \cos A = \frac{14}{9}$, we get
\begin{align*}
\sin C \sin B \cos A &= \frac{5}{3}.
\end{align*}
Squaring these equations and adding them, we get
\begin{align*}
2 \sin^2 B \sin^2 C \cos^2 A &= \frac{29}{72}.
\end{align*}
Taking the square root of both sides, we get
\begin{align*}
\sin B \sin C \cos A &= \frac{5}{6\sqrt{2}}.
\end{align*}
Substituting this back into the equation for $\sin C \sin B \cos A$, we get
\begin{align*}
\cos^2 A + \cos^2 B + 2 \sin C \sin A \cos B &= \frac{13}{27}.
