\text{On the first day:} \quad x_1 = x + \frac{1}{2} x = \frac{3}{2}x. \label{1}
\text{On the second day:} \quad x_2 = x_1 + \frac{1}{3} x_1 = x_1\frac{4}{3} = \frac{4}{3}x_1. \label{2}
\text{On the third day:} \quad x_3 = x_2 + \frac{1}{4} x_2 = x_2\frac{5}{4} = \frac{5}{4}x_2. \label{3}
Plug in the expressions for $\label{2} \and \label{3}$ into $\label{1}$:
\begin{align*}
x_1 &= \left(\frac{3}{2}x\right)\frac{4}{3}\frac{5}{4} \cdot \cdots \frac{n}{n-1}(n-1).
\end{align*}
Note that any factor in the expression above cancels out with a factor that was partially canceled by a previous factor; only the terms $\frac{3}{2}$ and $\frac{n}{n-1}$ remain.
Simplify the expression by multiplying all factors together:
\begin{align*}
x_1 &= x\frac{3}{2}\frac{4}{3}\frac{5}{4}\cdots \frac{n}{n-1}(n-1) \\
&= x(2)^{n-1}.
\end{align*}
We are told that the final length is 100 times the original length, so
\begin{align*}
100x &= x(2)^{n-1}.
\end{align*}
Cancel $x$ from both sides of the equation:
\begin{align*}
100 = (2)^{n-1}.
\end{align*}
A power of $2$ can only be $100$ if the number written as a power of $2$ is $2^4=16$ multiplied by $6$ with itself; so
\begin{align*}
(2)^{n-1} &= (2)^4(2)^3 \\
100 &= 2 + 3 + 1 + 4 + 5 + 6 \\
n-1 &= 20 \\
n &= 21.
\end{align*}
The answer is: 21

