We want to find the minimum value of 
\begin{eqnarray*}
(a+b+c+d)^{2} + (e+f+g+h)^{2}
\end{eqnarray*}  using the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality. 
We can rewrite the sum of squares as 
\begin{eqnarray*}
(a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2) + (e^2 + f^2 + g^2 + h^2).
\end{eqnarray*}
By the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality, we have 
\begin{eqnarray*}
(a^2 + b^2 + c^2 + d^2)(e^2 + f^2 + g^2 + h^2) \geq (ae + bf + cg + dh)^2 + (af + be + cg + dh)^2 + (ag + bf + ce + dh)^2 + (ah + bg + cf + dh)^2.
\end{eqnarray*}
Since $a, b, c, d, e, f, g, h$ are distinct elements in the set $\{-7, -5, -3, -2, 2, 4, 6, 13\}$, the minimum value of the expression is 
\begin{eqnarray*}
2^2 + 4^2 + 6^2 + 13^2 = 4 + 16 + 36 + 169 = 225.
\end{eqnarray*}
So the minimum possible value is $\boxed{225}.

