Among this substitutions we get:
\begin{align*}
75x^4 + ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + 12 &= 0, \\
\frac{75}{k}x^4 + \frac{a}{k}x^3 + \frac{b}{k}x^2 + \frac{c}{k}x + \frac{12}{k} &= 0, \\
12x^5 + dx^4 + ex^3 + fx^2 + gx + 75 &= 0, \\
\frac{12}{k}x^5 + d x^4 + e x^3 + f x^2 + g x + \frac{75}{k} &= 0.
\end{align*}
The first equality becomes:
\begin{equation}
75x^4 + 75k s^4 + a s^3 + a k s^2 + b s^2 + b k s + c s + 12k = 0, \tag{R01.1}
\end{equation}
The second equality becomes:
\begin{equation}
75s + \frac ap + \frac ab s + \frac ac s + \frac bk s^2 + \frac ak s^3 + \frac bk s + c s^2 + k = 0. \tag{R01.2}
\end{equation}
The third equality becomes:
\begin{equation}
\frac{12}{k}x^4 + d x^4 + e x^3 + f x^2 + g x + \frac{75}{k} = 0,
\end{equation}
which can be rewritten as:
\begin{equation}
\frac{12}{k}x^4 + 75s^4 + a b s^3 + b e s^2 + e a s + f s + g = 0, \tag{R01.3}
\end{equation}
where we substituted $\frac{x}{k^2} = s^4$ into (R01.3).
The fourth equality becomes:
\begin{equation}
\frac{12}{k}x^4 + d x^4 + e x^3 + f x^2 + g x + \frac{75}{k} = 0,
\end{equation}
which can be rewritten as:
\begin{equation}
\frac{12}{k}x^5 + \frac{12}{k}d x^3 + \frac{12}{k}e x^2 + \frac{12}{k}f x + \frac{75}{k} = 0,
\end{equation}
where we substituted $\frac{x}{k} = s^5$ into (R01.3).  For curiosity, here are the last three equations:
\begin{equation}
\tag{R01.4}
\left( \frac{12}{k} \right) s^5 + (d) s^4 + (a b) s^3 + (b e) s^2 + (e a) s + (f) s + (g) = 0,
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}
36 s^2 + k (3s + a b) + 5 (4 s + a e) + 3 (8 s + b e) + 2 (2 k s^3 + (f) s + g) = 0, \tag{R01.5}
\end{equation}
where all coefficients, including $k$, $s$, and $s^2$ are rational.
Now we seek a rational $k$ and a rational non-integer s that satisfy (R01.1)-(R01.5), which tells us $k$ is not rational.  Note that if $s = 0$ were a solution, the first equation would give $k = 0$ or would tell us $s \neq 0$, the second equation would give $s \neq 0$, the third equation would give $s \neq 0$, and (R01.5) also tells us we could not have $12 = 5 \times 0$ or $3 = 2 \times 0$.  It can be checked that $12 = 5 \times 0$ is clearly false.  Hence $s$ is not a solution of (R01.1)-(R01.5), and all coefficients of (R01.5) are integers.  Together with (R01.1) and (R01.2), these facts imply:
\begin{equation} \tag{R01.6}
\text{$s^4 + s^2 + s + 1 = 0$.}
\end{equation}
If $s$ is rational, (R01.6) tells us $\text{$s^2 = -s - 2$ is rational.}  If $s^2$ is rational and $s$ is not rational, the equation $s^4 + 2 = s^2$ has no rational solution.  Hence $s$ is irrational and, since (R01.6) indicates $s$ is an algebraic number of degree $4$ over the rational numbers, the minimal polynomial of $s$ over the rational numbers is
\begin{equation}
t^4 + t^2 + t + 1. \tag{R01.7}
\end{equation}
It follows from (R01.1) and (R01.2) that $75$ and $75k$ are roots of the minimal polynomial of $s$ (since (R01.1) tells us $75(x^4 + s^4) + (a k x^2 + b s^2 + c s + 12k) = 75 s^4 + (a k s^4 + b s^4 + c s + 12k) = 0$), which is impossible as $75$ is rational irreducible over the rationals.  So $k$ is irrational, and at least one of the equations (R01.3) or (R01.4) has rational coefficients.
If (R01.3) were to have rational coefficients, then multiplying (R01.3) by $12/k$ would give $-x$ as a root of the minimal polynomial of $s$ (since $x - (12/k)s^5 = (12/k)x^5 + (-x) = (12 k x^5 + 12)/(k^2) = -12 x^5/(k x) + 1/k^2 = 0$).  By uniquely minimal, the minimal polynomial of $s$ is therefore:
\begin{equation} \tag{R01.8}
36 t^4 + 21 t^3 + 2 t^2 - 3 t - 36.
\end{equation}
The polynomial (R01.8) cannot have 5 as a root, for otherwise $365 = 21$ would be true and if $s$ was $5/k$, (R01.2) would be satisfied.  For $5$ is an invariable root of (R01.8), (R01.6) gives us:
\begin{equation}
\frac 1 4t^4 + \frac 1 4t^2 + \frac 1 4t + 1 = 0, \tag{R01.9}
