We need to calculate the probability that in 5 trials, exactly 2 are definite green ("success") and 3 are definite non-green ("failure"). Since the marble is replaced after each draw, the number of green marbles available at each draw is constant (6 green, 4 non-green).

This is a binomial probability problem.
- Probability of choosing a green marble on a single draw: p = \dfrac{6}{6+4} = \dfrac{6}{10} = \dfrac{3}{5}.
- Probability of choosing a non-green marble on a single draw: q = 1-p = \dfrac{4}{10} = \dfrac{2}{5}.

Binomial formula:
P(X=k) = \binom{n}{k} p^{k} q^{n-k}

Here n=5, k=2:
P = \binom{5}{2} \left(\dfrac{3}{5}\right)^{2} \left(\dfrac{2}{5}\right)^{3}
= 10 \cdot \dfrac{9}{25} \cdot \dfrac{8}{125}
= 10 \cdot \dfrac{72}{3125}
= \dfrac{720}{3125}
= \dfrac{144}{625}

Numerically, \dfrac{144}{625} = 0.2304.

